// Kiddo School — EXTRA CLASS · Animal Categories:
// /extra-classes/animal-categories/learn/ lessons. One simple kid guide per
// animal family, in the set's tour order (pets & farm → crawly → feathers,
// fins & scales → big & wild → where animals live → giants & old wonders).
// The original flashcard sits LEFT with the drawing canvas; this guide sits
// RIGHT, written so a KID can follow it: what the family is, who belongs,
// how to spot or play them, step by step. Written fresh — the library pages
// in data-animal-categories.mjs answer WHAT the family is and WHAT the
// animals do; no text is shared between the two. The US/UK/CA pairs
// (ladybug/ladybird, cougar/mountain lion/puma, bunny/rabbit) are named in
// both flavors where the animals carry them. Age pills matter to the owner:
// every lesson names its audience honestly — the familiar families for
// threes, the habitat and concept cards for fours and fives.
export const lpContent={
 'pets':{
  title:'The Pets Lesson',one:'The animals that live at home with us.',
  seoTitle:'Pets Lesson for Kids — Dog, Cat, Rabbit and Hamster, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 3+: pets — the dog that wags, the cat that purrs, the rabbit that hops and the hamster that runs at night. How to care for a pet, step by step, and the gentle-hands rule.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches what pets are and how we care for them, one step at a time.',
  what:'A pet is an animal that lives in your home and depends on your family for everything — food, water, a safe place to sleep and love.',
  mean:'Care is the big idea: a pet cannot feed itself or call a doctor. When your child feeds the fish or pats the dog gently, they are practicing being the person another life depends on.',
  know:'Every pet needs fresh water every day, the right food (never too much), a clean place to sleep, and gentle hands — pets are never toys.',
  why:'Pets are usually a child&rsquo;s first friendship with another species, and the first time someone small is responsible for someone smaller.',
  dev:'Gentleness, routine-keeping (feeding happens even on tired days), reading another being&rsquo;s signals — a tail that wags is happy, a cat with flat ears wants space.',
  vocab:'pet, dog, cat, rabbit, hamster, feed, gentle, water bowl, cage, vet',
  teach:'1) Greet the pet by name, with quiet hands — pets recognize their people.\n2) Check the water bowl: full and clean comes first, every single day.\n3) Feed the right food, the right amount — a treat is a sometimes food, never dinner.\n4) Gentle strokes in the fur-growth direction; never grab, never squeeze, never tease.\n5) Notice and tell: if the pet seems hurt, tired or scared, say so — the grown-up is the vet-caller.',
  say:'"This is our pet. We keep the water full, we feed the right food, we are always gentle — and in return we get a friend who is always happy to see us. Gentle hands, happy pet!"',
  notice:'On the card, four pets sit in their squares: the dog with the wagging tail, the cat with the straight-up tail, the long-eared rabbit and the round hamster. Ask which one looks the most like a pet somebody in the family has known.',
  ask:'"What does every pet need every single day?" (water and food) "How do we touch a pet?" (gently) "What does the dog&rsquo;s wagging tail mean?" (happy to see you)',
  childSay:'Your child might line up every stuffed animal for a "vet check", fill a toy water bowl with invisible water a dozen times a day, or announce the hamster on the card is definitely named after their own.',
  real:'In the US, UK and Canada the family pet is often a child&rsquo;s first taste of daily responsibility — and a visit to the vet shows the same helpers-and-checks pattern as a visit to the doctor.',
  play:'Pet shop or vet clinic: stuffed animals queue up, one child is the vet with a cardboard-tube stethoscope, and every patient needs a name, a weight and a gentle exam before a sticker discharge.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we fill every day?" (the water bowl) "Can we squeeze the rabbit?" (no — gentle hands) "Who do we tell if the pet seems sick?" (a grown-up, who calls the vet)'},
 'farm-animals':{
  title:'The Farm Animals Lesson',one:'The crew that feeds the town.',
  seoTitle:'Farm Animals Lesson for Kids — Cow, Pig, Sheep and Horse, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 3+: farm animals — the cow gives milk, the pig is clever, the sheep grows wool and the horse works and rides. What each one gives us, and how to walk like the whole barnyard.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches what farm animals do for us, one animal at a time.',
  what:'Farm animals are the animals people keep on farms because they give us food and help: milk from the cow, wool from the sheep, and muscle power from the horse.',
  mean:'Thankfulness is the idea under the card: breakfast milk, wool sweaters and bacon-and-eggs all begin with an animal doing its job on a farm.',
  know:'Cows eat grass and turn it into milk; sheep grow wool that gets sheared like a haircut; pigs are among the smartest animals on the farm; horses sleep standing up.',
  why:'Understanding where food and clothes come from is a child&rsquo;s first economics lesson — things do not start at the store.',
  dev:'Sequencing (grass → cow → milk → breakfast), animal sounds as first phonics, and big-body movement through barnyard walks and gallops.',
  vocab:'farm, cow, milk, pig, sheep, wool, horse, barn, farmer, hay',
  teach:'1) Start with breakfast: "what did you have this morning? Milk? It began with a cow eating grass."\n2) Walk the barnyard on the card: cow says moo and gives milk, pig says oink, sheep says baa and grows wool, horse says neigh and gives rides.\n3) Match each animal to its gift: milk, wool, help and work.\n4) Act it out: chew like a cow, wallow like a pig, nibble like a sheep, gallop like a horse.\n5) End with the farmer: the person who feeds, cleans and cares for all of them — every day, no days off.',
  say:'"The farm is a team! The cow gives milk for our cereal, the sheep grows wool for sweaters, the horse pulls and carries, and the farmer takes care of them all. Thank you, farm animals!"',
  notice:'On the card, the cow has patches, the pig has a round snout, the sheep is a cloud of wool and the horse has a flowing mane. The Kiddo School sign sits on top — this is a farm that teaches.',
  ask:'"Who gives us milk?" (the cow) "Whose coat becomes a sweater?" (the sheep&rsquo;s wool) "Which animal is the cleverest on the farm?" (the pig — smarter than dogs at some games)',
  childSay:'Your child might moo through breakfast, insist the horse on the card is theirs, or shear a toy sheep with play scissors and knit nothing at all, proudly.',
  real:'Farms in the US, UK and Canada often open for visits in spring — lamb-feeding and cow-milking turn this card into a Saturday. A cow eats about eight hours a day: the grass has to keep coming.',
  play:'Barnyard parade: every family member picks a card animal, walks and sounds like it, and the farmer (rotating role) calls the animals home for supper. Milk the pretend cow — a rubber glove with pinpricks really works.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we get from the cow?" (milk) "What grows on the sheep?" (wool) "Who takes care of the farm animals?" (the farmer)'},
 'more-farm-animals':{
  title:'The More Farm Animals Lesson',one:'The barn has more than four doors.',
  seoTitle:'More Farm Animals Lesson for Kids — Goat, Donkey, Alpaca and Buffalo, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 3+: more farm animals — the goat that climbs, the donkey that carries and sings, the alpaca with the softest wool and the buffalo in the back field. What each one does on the farm.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the barn&rsquo;s second crew, one animal at a time.',
  what:'Beyond the famous four, farms keep specialist helpers: goats that clear brush and give milk, donkeys that carry and guard, alpacas that grow luxury wool, and buffalo that work the heavy fields.',
  mean:'Every animal has a specialty — the barn is a workplace where everyone&rsquo;s job fits, and the second crew does the jobs the famous four cannot.',
  know:'Goats can climb and stand on nearly anything; a donkey&rsquo;s hee-haw can be heard far away; alpaca wool is softer than many sweaters you own; buffalo are among the strongest farm animals.',
  why:'Meeting the "more" animals teaches children that the world is bigger than the famous examples — the second team matters as much as the first.',
  dev:'Comparing and contrasting (goat versus sheep, alpaca versus sheep), vocabulary breadth, and the idea of specialization — the right worker for the right job.',
  vocab:'goat, donkey, hee-haw, alpaca, buffalo, herd, climb, carry, wool, field',
  teach:'1) Line up the first crew&rsquo;s cards and ask "who is missing from the barn?" — enter the second crew.\n2) The goat: the climber — it stands on rocks, hay bales and sometimes the car roof. It also gives milk for cheese.\n3) The donkey: the carrier — it hauls loads and sings the loudest song in the barn. Donkeys also guard sheep from foxes: brave, not just loud.\n4) The alpaca: the soft one — its wool makes the coziest socks. Shearing day is a haircut, not a hurt.\n5) The buffalo: the strong one — heavy work in the back field, and a big warm coat for winter.',
  say:'"The barn has more friends than you think! The goat climbs, the donkey carries and sings hee-haw, the alpaca grows the fluffiest wool, and the buffalo is strong as a truck. Every animal has its job!"',
  notice:'On the card, the goat has little horns and a beard, the donkey has big ears and a mane that sticks straight up, the alpaca is a fluffy cloud with a long neck, and the buffalo has curved horns and shoulders taller than the rest.',
  ask:'"Which farm animal can climb almost anything?" (the goat) "Which one sings hee-haw?" (the donkey) "Whose wool makes the softest socks?" (the alpaca&rsquo;s)',
  childSay:'Your child might climb one stair and announce "I am the goat!", sing hee-haw at random intervals for a day, or pat the alpaca square and whisper "soft, so soft".',
  real:'Alpaca farms across the US, UK and Canada hold open shearing days, and petting zoos almost always keep a donkey and a goat — brush-a-goat sessions are a famous toddler joy.',
  play:'Farm job fair: deal the barn animals jobs written on cards — climb this, carry that, give wool, pull the plow — and match each animal to the job it does best. Wrong answers welcome: "the buffalo milks? Let&rsquo;s see…"',
  check:'Ask: "Who carries loads on its back?" (the donkey) "Who grows the soft wool?" (the alpaca) "What does the goat love to do?" (climb)'},
 'farm-birds':{
  title:'The Farm Birds Lesson',one:'Feathers, beaks and the morning chorus.',
  seoTitle:'Farm Birds Lesson for Kids — Chicken, Duck, Turkey and Goose, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 3+: farm birds — the chicken that lays eggs, the duck that paddles, the turkey that struts and the goose that honks. Who gives eggs, who guards the yard and how to walk like all four.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the birds of the barnyard, one feather at a time.',
  what:'Farm birds are the feathered farm workers: chickens lay the eggs, ducks paddle and forage, turkeys strut, and geese act as the barnyard&rsquo;s alarm system.',
  know:'Chickens lay eggs almost daily; ducks have waterproof feathers thanks to a special oil they spread from a spot near their tails; turkeys can change the color of their face skin; geese fly in V-formations and remember faces for years.',
  mean:'Birds are not just farm extras — they are a whole crew with different jobs: breakfast, pest control, watchdog duties and feather-down pillows.',
  why:'Eggs are probably the first food your child can trace to its source — the chicken did it, this morning, and the egg carton is the proof.',
  dev:'Sound discrimination (cluck, quack, gobble, honk), waddle-and-strut body play, and the first food-chain story: hen → egg → breakfast.',
  vocab:'chicken, hen, egg, duck, turkey, goose, honk, feather, beak, nest',
  teach:'1) Start at the egg carton in the fridge: "who made these? The hen — almost one a day!"\n2) The duck: waterproof feathers and paddle feet — it can swim all day and stays dry. Quack.\n3) The turkey: the strutter — its tail fans out like a parade wheel and it gobbles loud enough to hear across the farm.\n4) The goose: the guard goose — honks at strangers, hisses at intruders, and many farms use geese instead of watchdogs.\n5) Walk them all: waddle like a duck, strut like a turkey, march like a goose on patrol, scratch like a chicken.',
  say:'"Farm birds run the morning! The hen lays the eggs, the duck paddles the pond, the turkey gobbles and struts, and the goose honks at anything new. Feathers up, everyone!"',
  notice:'On the card, the chicken pecks in front, the duck sits low and round, the turkey shows a full fan of tail feathers, and the goose stands tallest with its long neck — the lookout.',
  ask:'"Who gives us eggs?" (the hen) "Which bird swims?" (the duck) "Which bird is the farm&rsquo;s alarm bell?" (the goose)',
  childSay:'Your child might gobble at the dinner table in November, waddle to the bath announcing "duck!", or check the fridge carton and thank the hens personally.',
  real:'Thanksgiving in the US and Canada centers the turkey — children who know this card can explain the whole bird. Goose-fair season and duck-pond visits make the rest of the card real.',
  play:'Egg-and-spoon farm: carry a boiled egg on a spoon across the "barnyard" without dropping it — the hen&rsquo;s delivery route. Add the honk: the goose patrol honks whenever a wobbly walker goes by.',
  check:'Ask: "Which farm bird swims?" (the duck) "Which one lays the eggs we eat?" (the chicken) "Which bird honks like an alarm?" (the goose)'},
 'insects':{
  title:'The Insects Lesson',one:'Six legs, big jobs.',
  seoTitle:'Insects Lesson for Kids — Butterfly, Bee, Ladybug and Dragonfly, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 3+: insects — six legs, two wings and big jobs. The butterfly&rsquo;s change, the bee&rsquo;s honey, the ladybug that eats pests (ladybird in Britain) and the dragonfly helicopter, with a bug-hunt walk.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the six-legged crew and their big jobs, one insect at a time.',
  what:'Insects are animals with six legs and a body in three parts — head, middle and tail-end. There are more insect kinds on Earth than all other animals added together.',
  mean:'Small does not mean unimportant: insects pollinate our food, recycle the garden and are lunch for birds and frogs — the world runs on small workers.',
  know:'All insects have six legs (count them!); the bee drinks nectar and makes honey; the ladybug — called ladybird in Britain — eats aphids that hurt plants; the dragonfly hunts other bugs mid-air.',
  why:'Insects are a child&rsquo;s first wild animals — findable in every garden, park and windowsill, no zoo required.',
  dev:'Counting to six on real creatures, careful observation without touching, and gentle-catch-and-release ethics: look, learn, let go.',
  vocab:'insect, six legs, butterfly, bee, honey, ladybug, ladybird, dragonfly, wings, antennae',
  teach:'1) The rule first: we look with our eyes, never our hands — bugs are wild friends, not toys.\n2) The butterfly: it hatched from a caterpillar that ate and grew, built a chrysalis, and came out with wings. A whole second life!\n3) The bee: it sips nectar and carries pollen between flowers — that is how fruit starts. Honey is its winter pantry.\n4) The ladybug: garden police — it eats hundreds of aphids. In Britain the same beetle is the ladybird; both names, one bug.\n5) The dragonfly: four wings that work separately — it can hover, fly backwards, and it eats mosquitoes. The pond&rsquo;s helicopter.',
  say:'"Insects have six legs and big jobs! The bee makes honey and carries pollen, the ladybug — ladybird, in Britain — guards the garden, the butterfly used to be a caterpillar, and the dragonfly flies like a tiny helicopter. Six legs, look and count!"',
  notice:'On the card, the butterfly sits wings-open, the bee hovers beside a flower, the ladybug shows its red shell with black spots, and the dragonfly stretches its four long wings in an X.',
  ask:'"How many legs does every insect have?" (six) "Which insect makes honey?" (the bee) "What did the butterfly used to be?" (a caterpillar)',
  childSay:'Your child might count six legs on every bug they meet for a week, patrol the garden announcing "ladybug police!", or refuse to squish a spider "because the ladybug said so".',
  real:'Spring and summer in the US, UK and Canada are insect seasons — a ten-minute garden hunt finds all four card insects most weeks. Beekeepers visit schools; honey straight from the comb is the tastiest show-and-tell.',
  play:'Bug-hunt bingo: draw a 2×2 card of your own with butterfly, bee, ladybug and dragonfly, and walk the block until every square is spotted. Eyes only, whisper voices, big checkmarks.',
  check:'Ask: "How many legs does an insect have?" (six) "What does the ladybug eat?" (the bugs that hurt plants) "How does the butterfly begin?" (as a caterpillar)'},
 'garden-creatures':{
  title:'The Garden Creatures Lesson',one:'The quiet workers under the leaves.',
  seoTitle:'Garden Creatures Lesson for Kids — Snail, Earthworm, Caterpillar and Grasshopper, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 3+: garden creatures — the snail with its house, the earthworm that helps plants grow, the caterpillar that will fly and the grasshopper that jumps. A rain-day hunt and the slow-look skill.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the garden&rsquo;s slow crew, one creature at a time.',
  what:'Garden creatures are the small animals that live among the plants: snails and earthworms that keep the soil healthy, caterpillars that become butterflies, and grasshoppers that leap among the stems.',
  mean:'A garden is a city, and these are its workers: some build the soil, some trim the leaves, some will fly away next month — every square of ground has staff.',
  know:'A snail carries its home and leaves a silver trail; worms have no eyes but feel vibrations; a caterpillar eats constantly because becoming a butterfly takes fuel; grasshoppers jump about twenty times their own length.',
  why:'Children who know the garden&rsquo;s workers stop squishing and start watching — the first step from "bugs are gross" to "bugs are colleagues".',
  dev:'Slow observation (the opposite of the bug-squish reflex), respect for small life, and cause-and-effect: worms → healthy soil → big tomatoes.',
  vocab:'snail, shell, trail, earthworm, soil, caterpillar, chrysalis, grasshopper, jump, antennae',
  teach:'1) The snail: find its shell — the house goes everywhere. Watch the silver trail: snails slide on their own slime so the rough ground never hurts them.\n2) The earthworm: no eyes, no legs, no lungs — it breathes through its skin, which is why it comes out in the rain. Its tunnels let air and water reach plant roots.\n3) The caterpillar: an eating machine — most of its life is lunch. Then it builds a chrysalis and changes into a butterfly or moth.\n4) The grasshopper: legs like springs — it jumps twenty times its body length. Try jumping one body length; be humbled politely.\n5) The garden rule: look closely, touch gently, and put every creature back exactly where it was found.',
  say:'"The garden has quiet workers! The snail carries its house, the worm digs tunnels for the roots to drink, the caterpillar eats and eats before it flies, and the grasshopper jumps and jumps. Walk soft — the workers are busy!"',
  notice:'On the card, the snail&rsquo;s shell spirals round, the earthworm curves like an S, the caterpillar shows its segments and little feet, and the grasshopper folds its long jumping legs like a coiled spring.',
  ask:'"What does the snail carry on its back?" (its house) "Why do worms come out when it rains?" (their tunnels flood; wet skin helps them breathe) "What will the caterpillar become?" (a butterfly or moth)',
  childSay:'Your child might build a "worm hotel" from a jar and leaves, follow a snail for an entire patient afternoon, or jump repeatedly from one paving stone to the next, measuring grasshopper style.',
  real:'After rain in any US, UK or Canadian garden, snails and worms appear on schedule. Butterfly houses and caterpillar kits let children watch the chrysalis change on the kitchen table — the card&rsquo;s biggest wow, live.',
  play:'Slow-race: choose a snail and a grasshopper toy — the snail must be moved one finger-width at a time, the grasshopper in jumps. Which reaches the lettuce first? Depends how patient the grasshopper is feeling.',
  check:'Ask: "Why are worms good for the garden?" (their tunnels help roots get air and water) "What does the caterpillar turn into?" (a butterfly) "Where does the snail live?" (in the shell on its back)'},
 'small-animals':{
  title:'The Small Animals Lesson',one:'Small bodies, big work.',
  seoTitle:'Small Animals Lesson for Kids — Mouse, Guinea Pig, Mole and Beaver, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 3+: small animals — the quick mouse, the squeaky guinea pig, the digging mole and the beaver that builds dams. Small bodies doing big work, with a cushion-dam game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches four small workers, one wonder at a time.',
  what:'Small animals are the little furred workers of the animal world: mice that squeeze through gaps the size of a coin, guinea pigs that talk in squeaks, moles that dig underground highways, and beavers that build dams bigger than a car.',
  mean:'Size is not strength: a beaver changes whole rivers, and a mouse survives everywhere on Earth except the poles. Small bodies run on clever plans.',
  know:'Mice can fit through coin-sized holes; guinea pigs squeak "wheek!" for food and are famous gentle pets; moles are nearly blind and dig with shovel-hands; beaver teeth never stop growing, so the chewing never stops either.',
  why:'Children love an underdog, and the small-animals card is a whole team of them — biggest engineering from the smallest engineers.',
  dev:'Respect for creatures usually dismissed, size-comparison math (how many mice tall is the fridge?), and cause-and-effect building play.',
  vocab:'mouse, tiny, guinea pig, wheek, mole, tunnel, beaver, dam, lodge, gnaw',
  teach:'1) The mouse: squeeze test — a mouse flattens its skull to fit through gaps the size of a coin. That is how it gets everywhere.\n2) The guinea pig: the talker — "wheek, wheek!" means food, and popcorning (little jumps) means joy. It is the card&rsquo;s most famous pet.\n3) The mole: underground digger — shovel-shaped hands, tiny eyes, tunnels with bedrooms and worm pantries.\n4) The beaver: the engineer — it fells trees with its teeth, piles them into dams, and the pond behind the dam becomes a whole new neighborhood for frogs, fish and ducks.\n5) The small rule: small does not mean weak — measure the beaver&rsquo;s work against the beaver&rsquo;s size.',
  say:'"Small animals, big work! The mouse fits through a coin-sized door, the guinea pig squeaks for snacks, the mole digs its own highways, and the beaver builds a house that changes the whole river!"',
  notice:'On the card, the mouse has big round ears, the guinea pig sits plump and calm, the mole holds its pink shovel-hands forward, and the beaver shows its flat paddle tail and orange-gnawing teeth.',
  ask:'"How small a hole can a mouse fit through?" (a coin-sized one) "Which small animal builds dams?" (the beaver) "What does the guinea pig say when it is snack time?" (wheek, wheek!)',
  childSay:'Your child might try to post a coin through a mouse-sized gap to test the claim, popcorn (jump for joy) exactly like a guinea pig at snack time, or dam a garden puddle with heroic sticks.',
  real:'Beaver dams are real road-trip sights across Canada and the northern US — look for the cone of sticks and the new pond behind it. Guinea pigs live in classrooms everywhere and squeak loudest when the fridge opens.',
  play:'Dam builders: pile couch cushions across a doorway and hold back a storm of rolled socks. The wave wins only if the dam leaks — the beaver always has one more stick.',
  check:'Ask: "Which small animal builds dams?" (the beaver) "What does the mole do all day?" (digs tunnels) "How does the guinea pig ask for food?" (wheek, wheek!)'},
 'amphibians':{
  title:'The Amphibians Lesson',one:'Animals that live two lives.',
  seoTitle:'Amphibians Lesson for Kids — Frog, Toad, Salamander and Newt, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 4–5: amphibians — animals that live in water AND on land. The frog&rsquo;s life cycle from egg to hopper, the warty toad, the tailed salamander and newt, with the metamorphosis dance.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the two-life animals, one change at a time.',
  what:'Amphibians are animals that live two lives — the word itself means "double life". They begin in water as eggs and tadpoles, then grow legs and lungs and hop onto land.',
  know:'Frogs sing ribbit and jump; toads walk and have warty, dry-ish skin; salamanders and newts keep their tails and prefer the damp and the rain; ALL amphibians have smooth, damp skin and must keep it wet.',
  mean:'Change is the big idea on this card: the same animal is first a swimming tadpole, then a hopping land animal — the most dramatic makeover in the animal kingdom.',
  why:'The frog life cycle is the clearest growth story a young child can watch — and ponds, puddles and spring nights are full of it.',
  dev:'Sequencing (egg → tadpole → froglet → frog), the big new word "amphibian" as a badge, and pond-side ethics: watch, never take, hands damp if a grown-up agrees to a gentle touch.',
  vocab:'amphibian, tadpole, frog, toad, salamander, newt, life cycle, pond, damp, ribbit',
  teach:'1) Start with the word: am-phib-i-an. Four beats — a big word for a big idea: two lives, one animal.\n2) Life one: eggs in the pond — jelly dots, hundreds of them. Then tadpoles: swimming dots with tails, no legs yet.\n3) The change: back legs sprout, then front legs, the tail shrinks away, and the gills become lungs. Life two: land.\n4) The land crew: the frog hops and sings, the toad walks and warts, the salamander and newt keep their tails and love the rain.\n5) The damp rule: amphibians breathe partly through their skin, so their skin must stay damp — which is why they sing loudest after rain.',
  say:'"Amphibians live two lives! First a swimming tadpole in the pond, then a hopping land animal. The frog says ribbit, the toad walks, the salamander and newt keep their tails — all of them keep damp skin!"',
  notice:'On the card, the frog sits tall and smooth, the toad is squat with bumpy skin, and the salamander and newt both curve their tails behind them. Count the life stages you can name from one card.',
  ask:'"What was the frog before it was a frog?" (a tadpole) "Do amphibians live in water or on land?" (both — two lives) "Why must their skin stay damp?" (they breathe through it a little)',
  childSay:'Your child might croak ribbit at every puddle for a month, practice the egg-tadpole-frog dance until it is perfect, or wear the word amphibian like a black belt.',
  real:'Spring ponds across the US, UK and Canada fill with frogspawn in March and April — a weekly pond visit shows the whole life cycle over about twelve weeks. Toads cross roads on rainy spring nights; some towns carry them across in buckets.',
  play:'The metamorphosis dance: egg (crouch small) → tadpole (wiggle with a tail-arm) → froglet (little hops) → frog (big jumps). Music optional; the landing ribbit is mandatory.',
  check:'Ask: "What does amphibian mean?" (double life) "What comes between egg and frog?" (tadpole) "Name a tailed amphibian." (salamander or newt)'},
 'birds':{
  title:'The Birds Lesson',one:'Feathers, beaks — and one surprise.',
  seoTitle:'Birds Lesson for Kids — Parrot, Penguin, Owl and Flamingo, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 3+: birds — feathers, beaks and two wings. The talking parrot, the swimming penguin, the night owl and the one-legged flamingo, with the Bird Olympics living-room games.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the feathered family and its famous surprise, one bird at a time.',
  what:'Birds are the feathered family: two wings, two legs, a beak, and feathers — the one coat no other animal wears. Most fly; a few have other plans.',
  know:'Parrots can learn words; penguins are birds that swim instead of fly — wings become flippers; owls hunt at night with silent wings and swivel heads; flamingos stand on one leg to save body heat and are pink because of the shrimp they eat.',
  mean:'The feather is the family flag: every bird wears them, from the parrot in the jungle to the penguin on the ice — and how each bird uses its wings is the story.',
  why:'Birds are the wild animals children see every single day — learning to look at them turns every window into a nature documentary.',
  dev:'Body imitation (balance, waddle, head-swivel), listening skills for bird calls, and the classification seed: feathers = bird, always.',
  vocab:'bird, feathers, beak, wings, parrot, penguin, owl, flamingo, perch, flock',
  teach:'1) The feather test: if it has feathers, it is a bird — feathers are the family uniform, no exceptions.\n2) The parrot: the talker — it copies sounds, and some learn hundreds of words. Its curved beak cracks the hardest nuts.\n3) The penguin: the surprise — a bird that gave flying back. Wings became flippers, and it "flies" underwater at speed.\n4) The owl: the night hunter — wings that fly silently, eyes that see in the dark, and a head that turns most of the way round.\n5) The flamingo: the balancer — one leg (try it for five seconds), and pink from the inside out: the pink comes from the tiny shrimp it eats.',
  say:'"Birds wear feathers — that is the family rule! The parrot talks, the penguin swims with flipper-wings, the owl hunts in the dark without a sound, and the flamingo stands on one leg. Try the leg. It is harder than it looks!"',
  notice:'On the card, the parrot perches with its hooked beak, the penguin stands straight like a little waiter, the owl has the widest eyes on the card, and the flamingo balances on its single long leg.',
  ask:'"What does every bird wear?" (feathers) "Which bird on the card cannot fly?" (the penguin — it swims) "Why is the flamingo pink?" (it eats pink shrimp)',
  childSay:'Your child might waddle to the bath penguin-style for a week, whisper who-who at every bedtime, or practice one-leg-standing until the family record falls.',
  real:'Parrot talks, zoo penguin parades and owl evenings at nature centers exist across the US, UK and Canada. Flamingos live in warm places — but the one-leg rule works on any playground.',
  play:'Bird Olympics: five events — flamingo stand (longest time), penguin waddle race, owl head-turn, parrot echo (repeat three words back), and the feather drop (which paper feather lands last?). Medals are bottle caps.',
  check:'Ask: "What makes a bird a bird?" (feathers) "How does the penguin travel?" (swimming with flippers) "What does the owl do at night?" (hunts, silently)'},
 'fish':{
  title:'The Fish Lesson',one:'Swimmers that breathe water.',
  seoTitle:'Fish Lesson for Kids — Salmon, Goldfish, Piranha and Catfish, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 3+: fish — gills, fins and scales. The salmon&rsquo;s long journey home, the goldfish bowl, the piranha&rsquo;s secret diet and the catfish whiskers, with the goldfish-wiggle game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the water-breathers, one fish at a time.',
  what:'Fish are the animals that live their whole lives underwater: they breathe water through gills, swim with fins and wear scales like armor.',
  know:'Gills are the fish&rsquo;s lungs — water goes in the mouth, over the gills, out the sides; salmon are born in rivers, swim to the ocean, and find their home river again years later; goldfish are the famous bowl fish (and remember more than people think); catfish feel for food with whiskers.',
  mean:'Different world, different breathing: we could not breathe water, a fish cannot breathe air — gills versus lungs is the first great animal split children can name.',
  why:'Fish are the first pets many children meet, and the aquarium is the first ecosystem they can watch whole: feeding, cleaning, the circle complete.',
  dev:'Early biology (gills, fins, scales as vocabulary), the journey story (salmon homing) as first epic narrative, and empathy-through-care for a bowl or tank.',
  vocab:'fish, gills, fins, scales, salmon, goldfish, piranha, catfish, whiskers, shoal',
  teach:'1) The gill demo: open your mouth wide and "breathe water" — in the mouth, out the sides. That is a fish&rsquo;s whole breathing life.\n2) The salmon: born in a river, swims to the sea, grows big — then swims home to the exact river bend where it hatched. No map. Scientists still argue how.\n3) The goldfish: the bowl companion — it learns when feeding time is, and its memory is measured in months, not seconds.\n4) The piranha: famous teeth — but it mostly eats seeds and fruit; the monster story is mostly movie.\n5) The catfish: the bottom-feeler — whiskers that taste the muddy water, finding dinner in the dark.',
  say:'"Fish breathe water! In the mouth, out the gills. The salmon swims across the whole ocean and still finds its home river, the goldfish knows dinner time, the piranha likes fruit more than fingers, and the catfish tastes the mud with its whiskers!"',
  notice:'On the card, the salmon arcs mid-jump, the goldfish has the biggest tail, the piranha shows its underbite and the catfish trails its whiskers below. Count the fins on each fish — every one is a steering wheel.',
  ask:'"What do fish breathe?" (water, through gills) "Which fish swims home to the river it was born in?" (the salmon) "Which fish has whiskers?" (the catfish)',
  childSay:'Your child might breathe-water-demonstrate at bath time for a week, adopt the goldfish as a personal client with a feeding schedule, or refuse the piranha monster story with a firm "seeds and fruit".',
  real:'Salmon runs in Canada and the US Northwest (and the UK&rsquo;s rivers) are visible from bridges in autumn — leaping fish, real and huge. Aquarium visits put every card fish within a pane of glass.',
  play:'Fish-y yoga: the goldfish wiggle (lie on back, wiggle all four fins), then fin-glides around the room with hands folded into a tail fin, bubble noises on. End with the salmon leap over a cushion waterfall.',
  check:'Ask: "How does a fish breathe?" (water through its gills) "What covers a fish&rsquo;s body?" (scales) "What does the catfish use whiskers for?" (tasting the water for food)'},
 'sea-creatures':{
  title:'The Sea Creatures Lesson',one:'Ocean treasures that are not fish.',
  seoTitle:'Sea Creatures Lesson for Kids — Seahorse, Starfish, Crab and Jellyfish, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 3+: sea creatures — the standing-swimmer seahorse, the regrowing starfish (sea star), the sideways crab and the floating jellyfish. Ocean movement games and the not-a-fish secret.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the ocean&rsquo;s non-fish wonders, one creature at a time.',
  what:'The sea holds animals that are not fish at all: the seahorse that swims standing up, the starfish (properly, sea star) that regrows arms, the crab in its shell suit, and the jellyfish, an animal that is mostly water and has no bones, brain or heart.',
  know:'Seahorse daddies carry the babies; a sea star can regrow a whole lost arm; crabs walk sideways because their knees only bend that way; jellyfish pulse their umbrellas and sting with drifting tentacles — look, never touch, even washed-up ones.',
  mean:'Life invents every shape: no bones, no face, no legs, ten legs, standing-up swimming — the ocean proves there is more than one way to be an animal.',
  why:'These are the beach-combing treasures: children who can name them see the shoreline as a treasure map instead of just sand.',
  dev:'Shape vocabulary (spiral, fan, star, umbrella), safe-beach ethics, and the classification jolt: no fish on this card at all.',
  vocab:'seahorse, sea star, starfish, crab, claws, jellyfish, tentacles, tide pool, float, shell',
  teach:'1) The seahorse: swims standing up, tail curled around seagrass like a hand holding a rope — and it is the dad who carries the babies in his pouch.\n2) The sea star: five arms, hundreds of tiny feet underneath, and the superpower of regrowth — lose an arm, grow it back.\n3) The crab: the side-stepper — its knees bend only sideways, so sideways it walks, shell suit included. Claws are its tools and its doorbell.\n4) The jellyfish: no bones, no brain, no heart — just a pulsing umbrella and drifting tentacles. Some glow in the dark.\n5) The beach rule: tide pools are windows, not souvenir shops — look close, touch nothing with tentacles, and put every stone back.',
  say:'"Sea creatures are the ocean&rsquo;s oddballs — none of them are fish! The seahorse swims standing up, the sea star regrows its arms, the crab walks sideways, and the jellyfish is a floating umbrella. Look in every tide pool!"',
  notice:'On the card, the seahorse curls its tail, the starfish spreads all five arms, the crab raises both claws, and the jellyfish drags its long tentacles below the bell.',
  ask:'"Which one is NOT a fish — crab, salmon, jellyfish?" (trick: crab and jellyfish are not fish!) "Who carries the seahorse babies?" (the father) "How does a crab walk?" (sideways)',
  childSay:'Your child might sideways-scuttle through every doorway for a week, check every beached "jellyfish" at arm&rsquo;s length like a lifeguard, or insist on re-naming the starfish a sea star (correctly).',
  real:'Tide-pooling on the US, UK and Canadian coasts finds all four within a few meters at low tide. Aquariums keep seahorse nurseries and jellyfish tanks that glow — the card comes alive behind glass.',
  play:'Ocean movement game: crab scuttle across the room, jellyfish float (arms dangle, slow pulses), seahorse hold (one spot, gripping an imaginary seagrass), sea star stretch (five arms — arms and legs plus... work it out as a family).',
  check:'Ask: "What is a starfish really called?" (a sea star — and it is not a fish) "Why do crabs walk sideways?" (their knees only bend that way) "Is a jellyfish safe to touch?" (no — look, never touch)'},
 'reptiles':{
  title:'The Reptiles Lesson',one:'Scales, sun and slow warm-ups.',
  seoTitle:'Reptiles Lesson for Kids — Snake, Crocodile, Chameleon and Lizard, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 4–5: reptiles — scaly sunbathers. The legless snake, the crocodile&rsquo;s mighty jaw, the color-changing chameleon and the push-up lizard, with reptile charades and the sunshine rule.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the scaly family and its sunshine secret, one reptile at a time.',
  what:'Reptiles are the scaly animals — snakes, crocodiles, chameleons, lizards and their kin — with dry, scaled skin and bodies that warm up in the sun.',
  know:'Snakes have no legs at all and "hear" through their jawbones; crocodiles have the strongest bite ever measured in a living animal; chameleons change color to talk and to hide; lizards do push-ups — a real signal that means "this rock is mine".',
  mean:'Cold-blooded is the key idea, said kindly: reptiles run on sunshine the way we run on food — the morning basking session IS their breakfast.',
  why:'Reptiles are the closest living look at the dinosaur age — same scales, same rules — and children adore the connection.',
  dev:'Understanding body-temperature differences across animals, signal-reading (push-ups and color changes are messages), and safe-distance respect for wild reptiles.',
  vocab:'reptile, scales, cold-blooded, bask, snake, crocodile, chameleon, lizard, camouflage, jaw',
  teach:'1) The scale rule: reptiles wear dry scales — no fur, no feathers. Touch a toy snake: that texture is the family trademark.\n2) The sunshine rule: reptiles wake up cold and bask to warm their engines — a morning sunbathe is their breakfast.\n3) The snake: zero legs, and it moves anyway — s-shaped ripples. It "hears" vibrations through its jaw on the ground.\n4) The crocodile: the jaw — bite force champion, and it lurks like a floating log with eyes. Respect from a distance, always.\n5) The chameleon and the lizard: the chameleon changes color to send messages and to vanish; the lizard does push-ups — a real signal that means "this rock is mine".',
  say:'"Reptiles wear scales and run on sunshine! The snake slithers with no legs at all, the crocodile has the strongest bite, the chameleon changes color to talk, and the lizard does push-ups on its rock. Morning sun, then breakfast!"',
  notice:'On the card, the snake curves through its whole square, the crocodile shows its toothy grin, the chameleon grips a branch with its mitten feet and curled tail, and the lizard does its push-up on a warm rock.',
  ask:'"How does a reptile warm up?" (basking in the sun) "Which reptile has no legs?" (the snake) "What does the chameleon change?" (its color — to talk and to hide)',
  childSay:'Your child might do lizard push-ups on any warm patio stone, slither across the carpet with perfect S-arms, or explain to a grandparent that cold-blooded means "sun-powered, not grumpy".',
  real:'Zoo reptile houses have basking lamps that show the sunshine rule live — look for the lizard parked under the light at opening time. In warm US states, lizards do push-ups on garden walls all summer.',
  play:'Reptile charades: slither, chomp slow-motion, color-change walk (cushion to cushion, changing your "color" word at each), and the push-up finish. The audience guesses; the rock stays warm.',
  check:'Ask: "What covers a reptile&rsquo;s skin?" (dry scales) "Why do they bask?" (to warm their bodies) "What does the lizard&rsquo;s push-up mean?" ("this rock is mine!")'},
 'big-animals':{
  title:'The Big Animals Lesson',one:'The heavyweights of the wild.',
  seoTitle:'Big Animals Lesson for Kids — Zebra, Rhinoceros, Hippopotamus and Gorilla, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 4–5: big animals — the striped zebra, the horned rhino, the water-loving hippo and the chest-beating gorilla. Stripe patterns, hippo sunscreen and gorilla strength, with measuring games.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the animal kingdom&rsquo;s heavyweights, one giant at a time.',
  what:'Big animals are the giants that walk on grass and leaves: the zebra, the rhinoceros, the hippopotamus and the gorilla — every one of them bigger than a person, and every one of them eating mostly plants.',
  know:'No two zebras have the same stripes; a rhino&rsquo;s horn is made of the same stuff as your fingernails; hippos make their own pink sunscreen goo and are among the most dangerous animals in Africa; gorillas are the strongest of the great apes and beat their chests to be heard, not to start fights.',
  mean:'Big runs on plants: the biggest land animals are vegetarians — grass and leaves build gorillas and hippos, not steaks.',
  why:'Children measure themselves against the world constantly, and these four give the most exciting rulers of all — bigger than the car, longer than the couch.',
  dev:'Measurement play (longer than, taller than, heavier than), pattern recognition (stripes as fingerprints), and the plant-power food chain.',
  vocab:'big, heavy, zebra, stripes, rhinoceros, horn, hippopotamus, gorilla, chest, giant',
  teach:'1) The zebra: stripes like fingerprints — no two alike. Scientists think the stripes confuse biting flies.\n2) The rhino: the horn is keratin — fingernail stuff, growing all its life. Horns are why rhinos need rangers to guard them.\n3) The hippo: lives half in water to stay cool, and oozes its own pink sunscreen. Opens its mouth wide as a warning — "this is my stretch of river".\n4) The gorilla: the gentle heavyweight — eats plants, naps a lot, and beats its chest because the chest is a drum that carries through the forest.\n5) The measure-off: lie down next to each, mentally — every animal on this card is longer than a bathtub.',
  say:'"Big animals! The zebra&rsquo;s stripes are like your fingerprints — no two zebras the same! The rhino&rsquo;s horn is fingernail-stuff, the hippo makes its own sunscreen, and the gorilla is so strong and so gentle. The biggest animals eat plants!"',
  notice:'On the card, the zebra wears its stripe suit, the rhino stands square with its horn, the hippo shows the wide jaw and the gorilla sits like a wise uncle. Which one is longest? Measure with your eyes.',
  ask:'"What are zebra stripes like?" (fingerprints — all different) "What is a rhino&rsquo;s horn made of?" (keratin, like fingernails) "Why does the gorilla beat its chest?" (it is a drum — a sound message)',
  childSay:'Your child might tape-stripe a white t-shirt into a zebra, lie beside the sofa announcing "I am longer than the hippo — probably", or beat a gentle chest-drum hello every morning.',
  real:'Zoos everywhere keep all four; a zoo-visit checklist of this card is a full afternoon. Hippos in the wild spend up to sixteen hours a day in the water — the river is their bedroom and their fridge.',
  play:'Big-animal measuring: string or ribbon cuts to zebra, rhino, hippo and gorilla lengths, laid down the hallway. Which is longer than the family? Walk the line and count footsteps.',
  check:'Ask: "What do the biggest land animals mostly eat?" (plants — grass and leaves) "What are zebra stripes like?" (fingerprints — all different) "What does the hippo make?" (its own pink sunscreen)'},
 'safari-animals':{
  title:'The Safari Animals Lesson',one:'The savanna&rsquo;s greatest hits.',
  seoTitle:'Safari Animals Lesson for Kids — Lion, Elephant, Giraffe and Monkey, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 3+: safari animals — the roaring lion, the elephant&rsquo;s amazing trunk, the giraffe&rsquo;s tall neck and the swinging monkey. Safari is Swahili for journey, with a living-room jeep ride.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the safari&rsquo;s famous four, one stop at a time.',
  what:'Safari means journey in Swahili, a language of East Africa — and the safari&rsquo;s classic sightings are the lion, the elephant, the giraffe and the monkey.',
  know:'Lions sleep up to twenty hours a day and roar so loud it carries eight kilometers; an elephant&rsquo;s trunk has some 40,000 muscles and works like a hand; the giraffe&rsquo;s neck has the same seven bones as yours, just much longer; monkeys use their tails like an extra hand.',
  mean:'The savanna is a stage with roles: the resting king, the trunk-handed giant, the treetop diner and the acrobat — every animal has its act.',
  why:'Safari animals are the poster-children of the wild — and the word safari itself is a child&rsquo;s first travel vocabulary.',
  dev:'Big-movement play (prowl, trunk-swing, tall-stretch, swing), sound recognition (roar, rumble), and geography seed: Africa on the map where these friends live.',
  vocab:'safari, savanna, lion, roar, elephant, trunk, giraffe, monkey, swing, pride',
  teach:'1) Say the word: safari — Swahili for journey. Every safari is a journey to meet animals on their own ground.\n2) The lion: sleeps most of the day in the shade, hunts mostly in the dark — and the ROAR carries eight kilometers. Even kings need naps.\n3) The elephant: the trunk is a nose and a hand — it can pick up one peanut or push over a tree, and it says hello by wrapping trunks.\n4) The giraffe: the neck has the same seven bones as your neck — stretched. It eats from the treetops nobody else can reach.\n5) The monkey: the acrobat — the tail grips while the hands grab. Troops chatter louder than the birds.',
  say:'"Safari means journey! The lion roars so loud you can hear it far away, the elephant says hello with its trunk, the giraffe eats from the very top of the tree, and the monkey swings and swings. Climb in — the jeep leaves now!"',
  notice:'On the card, the lion sits with its mane like a crown, the elephant lifts its trunk, the giraffe stretches past the top of its square, and the monkey dangles by one arm. Which one looks the sleepiest?',
  ask:'"What does safari mean?" (journey) "Which animal has a trunk that works like a hand?" (the elephant) "How many neck bones does a giraffe have?" (seven — same as you)',
  childSay:'Your child might build a jeep from kitchen chairs and issue tickets, trumpet hello through a cupped hand at every goodbye, or stretch on tiptoe measuring everything "giraffe-high".',
  real:'Every large zoo keeps all four; the elephant&rsquo;s trunk-hello is the card&rsquo;s real-world showstopper. In Kenya and Tanzania, real safaris ride past sleeping lion prides daily.',
  play:'Safari drive: chairs in a row, one driver, and the animals appear at each window — prowl like a lion (slow and low), walk with an arm-trunk, tiptoe tall like a giraffe, swing like a monkey. Binoculars from toilet rolls complete it.',
  check:'Ask: "What language gives us the word safari?" (Swahili — it means journey) "What does the elephant use its trunk for?" (like a hand — pick up, spray, say hello) "Who sleeps most of the day?" (the lion)'},
 'wild-cats':{
  title:'The Wild Cats Lesson',one:'Your kitten&rsquo;s bigger cousins.',
  seoTitle:'Wild Cats Lesson for Kids — Tiger, Leopard, Cougar and Lynx, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 4–5: wild cats — the striped tiger, the tree-carrying leopard, the cougar (also mountain lion, also puma) and the paintbrush-eared lynx. Quiet-paws games and the one-name-three-names secret.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the house cat&rsquo;s wild cousins, one cat at a time.',
  what:'Wild cats are the big members of the cat family: the tiger, the leopard, the cougar and the lynx — same whiskers, same quiet feet, much bigger paws.',
  know:'The tiger is the biggest cat on Earth and the only big cat that loves water; the leopard can carry its dinner up a tree; the cougar goes by three names — cougar, puma and mountain lion; the lynx has ear-tufts like paintbrushes and huge snowshoe paws.',
  mean:'Same family, different jobs: stripes for the grass, rosettes for the forest shadows, plain tan for the mountains — every cat&rsquo;s coat is cut for its home.',
  why:'Children meet this card as an extension of their own pets: the house cat&rsquo;s stalking, pouncing and purring all make sense next to its wild cousins.',
  dev:'Pattern matching (stripes vs rosettes vs plain), movement play (stalking, leaping), and the geography seed: tigers in Asia, cougars in the Americas, lynx across the north.',
  vocab:'wild cat, tiger, leopard, cougar, puma, mountain lion, lynx, paws, prowl, whiskers',
  teach:'1) Start with the pet: does your cat stalk, pounce, purr? Every trick it knows, its wild cousins know bigger.\n2) The tiger: the giant — stripes like a barcode, and unlike most cats it loves a swim.\n3) The leopard: the tree-climber — it can carry a whole dinner up a tree by its teeth, away from every thief.\n4) The cougar: the name-shape-shifter — cougar, puma, mountain lion: three names, one cat, all correct. It purrs like a giant house cat.\n5) The lynx: the snowshoe cat — huge paws that work like snow boots, and ear-tufts like paintbrush tips.',
  say:'"Wild cats — your kitten&rsquo;s big cousins! The tiger is the biggest cat in the world and it swims, the leopard carries dinner up a tree, the cougar has three names and all of them are right, and the lynx wears snowshoes on its paws!"',
  notice:'On the card, the tiger shows its stripes, the leopard its rosette spots, the cougar is plain tan and muscular, and the lynx wears ear-tufts and a ruff. Match each coat to its hiding place.',
  ask:'"Which wild cat swims?" (the tiger) "What are the cougar&rsquo;s three names?" (cougar, puma, mountain lion) "Why does the lynx have big paws?" (snowshoes for walking on snow)',
  childSay:'Your child might announce there are three cats in the yard when one cougar walks by, prowl the hallway on quiet cat-feet, or hold a pencil-tip pose as "lynx ears".',
  real:'Cougars still live wild across much of the US and Canada (as mountain lions), and lynx in the snowy north — most people never see one, which is exactly how the cats prefer it. Zoos keep tigers and leopards within a pane of glass.',
  play:'Quiet-paws challenge: cross the room from couch to kitchen without one sound — wild cats land soft. Then one huge tiger leap, a leopard "carry" (a stuffed toy gripped in the teeth, gently), and a proud purr at the finish.',
  check:'Ask: "How many names does the cougar have?" (three — cougar, puma, mountain lion) "Which cat is the biggest in the world?" (the tiger) "What do leopards do with their dinner?" (carry it up a tree)'},
 'primates':{
  title:'The Primates Lesson',one:'Our cousins with clever hands.',
  seoTitle:'Primates Lesson for Kids — Chimpanzee, Orangutan, Baboon and Lemur, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 4–5: primates — our own family. The tool-using chimpanzee, the long-armed orangutan, the savanna baboon and the ring-tailed lemur, with clever-hands challenges.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the monkey-and-ape family — the family people belong to, one cousin at a time.',
  what:'Primates are the family of monkeys, apes — and people. We all share clever gripping hands, front-facing eyes and big family lives.',
  know:'Chimpanzees make and use tools — sticks fished into termite mounds; orangutans have arms longer than their legs and rarely touch the ground; baboons live in troops on the savanna; lemurs live only on Madagascar and wave their ringed tails.',
  mean:'Family is the idea: primates learn from their mothers, play as children, and solve problems with their hands — the same kit you are using to read this card.',
  why:'Meeting the primates is meeting our own reflection — the first "we are animals too" a child can see with their own eyes.',
  dev:'Fine-motor practice (the primate superpower is the gripping hand), empathy and family-structure talk, and the big science seed: humans are primates.',
  vocab:'primate, ape, monkey, chimpanzee, orangutan, baboon, lemur, troop, hands, cousins',
  teach:'1) Start with the child&rsquo;s own hand: thumb wrapped around a crayon — THAT grip is the primate signature.\n2) The chimpanzee: the toolmaker — it trims a stick, pokes it into a termite mound, and pulls out lunch. It also hugs and holds hands.\n3) The orangutan: the long arm — it swings and climbs, barely touching the ground, and builds a new nest of leaves to sleep in every night.\n4) The baboon: the savanna troop-member — big groups, loud politics, and cheeks it can fill with food like shopping bags.\n5) The lemur: the Madagascan — found wild nowhere else on Earth, and the ringed tail is a flag it carries upright on walks.',
  say:'"Primates are our cousins! Clever hands, clever families. The chimpanzee fishes for termites with a stick-tool, the orangutan builds a fresh leaf-nest every night, the baboon lives in a big noisy troop, and the lemur waves its ringed tail!"',
  notice:'On the card, the chimpanzee sits with its hands forward, the orangutan hangs by long arms, the baboon shows its long muzzle, and the lemur raises the famous black-and-white rings.',
  ask:'"Are people primates too?" (yes — the same family) "Which primate uses a stick as a tool?" (the chimpanzee) "Where do lemurs live wild?" (only on Madagascar)',
  childSay:'Your child might fish "termites" (pom-poms) out of a chair-leg "mound" with a stick-tool for a whole afternoon, build a leaf-nest of cushions every night, or walk with an upright tail-scarf, lemur style.',
  real:'Zoo ape houses show the hand-grip and the tool use live — the chimpanzee termite-fishing is the card&rsquo;s most repeatable wow. Madagascar is the only lemur country: find it on the map together.',
  play:'Clever-hands minute: stack five blocks, thread three big buttons, turn three pages one at a time, then undo one zipper. Every task is a primate skill — the family superpower is the hand.',
  check:'Ask: "What is the primate superpower?" (clever gripping hands) "Which primate makes stick-tools?" (the chimpanzee) "Are people primates?" (yes)'},
 'forest-animals':{
  title:'The Forest Animals Lesson',one:'Home among the trees.',
  seoTitle:'Forest Animals Lesson for Kids — Bear, Fox, Deer and Squirrel, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 4–5: forest animals — the winter-sleeping bear, the white-tipped fox, the quiet deer and the acorn-banking squirrel. Habitats begin here, with an acorn hide-and-seek.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the forest&rsquo;s four residents, one tree at a time.',
  what:'Forest animals are the creatures that live in the world&rsquo;s woodlands: the bear, the fox, the deer and the squirrel — a neighborhood built of trunks, leaves and quiet paths.',
  know:'Bears spend the winter in a long sleepy rest called torpor; foxes have white tail-tips and cat-like vertical pupils; deer walk on two toes and can freeze mid-step for a minute; squirrels bury thousands of acorns and remember most of them — the forgotten ones grow into trees.',
  mean:'Habitat is the new big idea: an animal is built for its home, and the forest builds them all — fur for winter, hooves for leaves, cheeks for acorns.',
  why:'The forest is often the first habitat a child walks through — this card is the trail map for it.',
  dev:'Habitat thinking (the first of the where-they-live cards), observation on real woodland walks, and food-storage science through squirrel play.',
  vocab:'forest, woodland, bear, fox, deer, squirrel, acorn, den, torpor, antlers',
  teach:'1) The bear: eats all summer and autumn, then sleeps the winter off in a den — a long doze with a spring alarm clock.\n2) The fox: the clever one — white tail-tip, quick pounce into snow, ears that hear a mouse under the white. City foxes use the same tricks on bins.\n3) The deer: the quiet one — walks on two toes, freezes when worried, and the males grow new antlers every year.\n4) The squirrel: the banker — buries acorns everywhere, remembers most, and the lost ones become oak trees. Squirrels plant forests.\n5) The walk rule: move slowly, talk quietly, and look UP as well as down — the forest has two floors.',
  say:'"The forest is a home! The bear sleeps all winter, the fox pounces in the snow, the deer walks on quiet hooves, and the squirrel buries acorns — and the ones it forgets grow into whole oak trees!"',
  notice:'On the card, the bear stands big and round, the fox shows its white tail-tip, the deer carries small antlers, and the squirrel holds its acorn with both hands. Two animals have antlers or horns — find them.',
  ask:'"What does the bear do all winter?" (a long sleepy rest in a den) "What does the squirrel do with acorns?" (buries them — and plants forests by accident) "What grows on a male deer every year?" (new antlers)',
  childSay:'Your child might bury "acorns" in every plant pot and remember most of them, sleep through a whole Saturday announcing torpor, or freeze mid-step like a deer when the doorbell rings.',
  real:'Woodland trails across the US, UK and Canada hold all four — dawn and dusk are the seeing hours. The squirrel&rsquo;s oak-planting is real: many forests grew from forgotten acorns.',
  play:'Acorn hide-and-seek: hide five acorns (pebbles, paper balls) around one room, then let the squirrel hunt. The bear&rsquo;s job: sleep through the whole thing on the couch, and wake for exactly one snack.',
  check:'Ask: "Who plants new oak trees?" (the squirrel, by forgetting acorns) "What is a bear&rsquo;s winter sleep called?" (torpor — a long doze) "How does the deer move?" (quietly, on two toes)'},
 'desert-animals':{
  title:'The Desert Animals Lesson',one:'Clever tricks for the hottest home.',
  seoTitle:'Desert Animals Lesson for Kids — Camel, Meerkat, Scorpion and Fennec Fox, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 4–5: desert animals — the camel&rsquo;s fat humps, the meerkat guard duty, the glowing scorpion and the fennec fox&rsquo;s air-conditioner ears. Heat-beating tricks and meerkat watch.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the desert&rsquo;s survivalists, one trick at a time.',
  what:'Desert animals live where rain almost never comes — and they survive with brilliant tricks: fat-storing humps, guard towers, glow-in-the-dark armor and air-conditioner ears.',
  know:'A camel&rsquo;s humps store fat (not water) and it can walk for days; meerkats take turns standing guard; scorpions glow under UV light; the fennec fox&rsquo;s giant ears release body heat — built-in cooling.',
  mean:'Adaptation is the idea: where the home is hard, the animals get creative — every desert animal is an engineer of its own comfort.',
  why:'The desert is the most dramatic habitat-lesson on the deck: nothing survives there by accident, so every card square is a working invention.',
  dev:'Cause-and-effect thinking (big ears = cool blood), turn-taking games, and respect-for-distance with stinging neighbors.',
  vocab:'desert, camel, hump, meerkat, guard, scorpion, fennec fox, ears, adapt, shade',
  teach:'1) The camel: the humps are fat pantries, not water tanks — food for a long journey. Long lashes and closing nostrils keep the sand out.\n2) The meerkat: the lookout — one stands tall while the others dig, and the guard swaps out every few minutes. Teamwork with a whistle vocabulary.\n3) The scorpion: the armored hunter — curls its sting-tail over its back, and glows blue-green under UV torchlight. Look from a respectful distance: the sting is real.\n4) The fennec fox: the ears — huge, and full of blood vessels close to the skin: heat escapes through them like a radiator.\n5) The desert rule: be active at dawn and dusk, rest in the shade at noon — the animals all follow it, and so do sensible desert travelers.',
  say:'"The desert is hot, hot, hot — but its animals have tricks! The camel carries fat in its humps for the long walk, the meerkats take turns on guard, the scorpion glows in the dark, and the fennec fox cools itself with its giant ears!"',
  notice:'On the card, the camel stands tall with its humps, the meerkat sits upright on lookout, the scorpion curls its tail, and the fennec fox holds its enormous ears wide. Which trick cools which animal?',
  ask:'"What is inside a camel&rsquo;s humps?" (fat — food for the journey) "What do meerkats do in shifts?" (stand guard) "How does the fennec fox stay cool?" (heat escapes through its big ears)',
  childSay:'Your child might take guard duty on the sofa arm for exactly ten seconds at a time, fan their ears with their hands declaring "fennec cooling system!", or refuse noon walks forever on desert-rule grounds.',
  real:'Zoo desert houses show all four, and UV-torch scorpion nights are a real park-ranger event in the US Southwest. Camel treks run in deserts on three continents — the sway is the memorable part.',
  play:'Meerkat watch: one lookout on a cushion tower while the others dig in a sandpit or rice bin — swap the guard every ten seconds, exactly like the real mob. A whistle means everyone freezes.',
  check:'Ask: "What does the camel store in its humps?" (fat) "Why does the fennec fox have huge ears?" (to release heat and stay cool) "When are desert animals busiest?" (dawn and dusk, resting at noon)'},
 'arctic-animals':{
  title:'The Arctic Animals Lesson',one:'The thickest coats on Earth.',
  seoTitle:'Arctic Animals Lesson for Kids — Polar Bear, Seal, Penguin and Walrus, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 4–5: arctic animals — the swimming polar bear, the sliding seal, the tuxedoed penguin and the tusked walrus. The blubber-glove experiment and the north-south secret.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the ice&rsquo;s warmest residents, one coat at a time.',
  what:'Arctic animals live at the top of the world, where everything is ice and wind — and they wear the thickest coats on Earth: fur, blubber and feathers doing full-time insulation work.',
  know:'Polar bears swim for kilometers and their "white" fur is actually hollow and clear; seals haul out on ice to rest and slide on their bellies; penguins live at the SOUTH pole too — but never meet polar bears, who live only in the north; walruses pull out of the water with two long tusks.',
  mean:'Warmth is engineering: blubber is a wetsuit, hollow fur is a down jacket, and huddling penguins share body heat like a rotating group hug.',
  why:'The arctic is the deck&rsquo;s most dramatic contrast-card — and its animals carry the climate conversation in kid language: the ice is their home, and it is changing.',
  dev:'Temperature science through the blubber experiment, direction talk (north pole, south pole), and the great never-meet fact as first map work.',
  vocab:'arctic, ice, polar bear, seal, penguin, walrus, blubber, tusk, den, huddle',
  teach:'1) The polar bear: the giant swimmer — its fur looks white but every hair is hollow and clear; the sun does the painting. It hunts from the ice.\n2) The seal: the slider — wiggle-hops on ice, swims like a torpedo, and naps in the open because the cold keeps rivals away.\n3) The penguin: the tuxedo — feathers over fat, and the huddle: thousands of penguins rotate so everyone gets a warm turn in the middle.\n4) The walrus: the tusk-puller — the tusks are its ice-axes, hooking out of the water onto the floe.\n5) The big secret: penguins and polar bears never meet — penguins live around the south, polar bears around the north. The map splits them forever.',
  say:'"Arctic animals wear the thickest coats on Earth! The polar bear swims in icy seas, the seal slides on its belly, the penguin huddles with its friends, and the walrus pulls out of the water on two tusks. And the biggest secret: penguins and polar bears never meet!"',
  notice:'On the card, the polar bear walks tall, the seal lies flat, the penguin stands in its tuxedo and the walrus shows both tusks. Two of these four live at the south pole — which?',
  ask:'"What is a polar bear&rsquo;s fur really like?" (hollow, clear hairs) "How do penguins stay warm?" (feathers, fat, and the rotating huddle) "Do polar bears eat penguins in the wild?" (no — they live at opposite poles)',
  childSay:'Your child might waddle-huddle with every stuffed penguin in rotation, slide belly-first off every couch "like a seal", or correct the movies: "bears and penguins NEVER meet."',
  real:'The blubber-glove experiment (a bag of shortening inside a bag, one hand in ice) shows the science in ten seconds — a famous classroom demo. Penguin colonies in zoos and aquariums bring the huddle indoors.',
  play:'Blubber experiment: one bare hand in ice water — brrr! The other inside a "blubber glove" — warm! Then the penguin huddle: the family stands back-to-back rotating slowly, everyone gets a middle turn.',
  check:'Ask: "What keeps arctic animals warm?" (thick fur, feathers and blubber) "Where do penguins live — north or south?" (south; polar bears live north) "What does the walrus use its tusks for?" (pulling itself onto the ice)'},
 'australian-animals':{
  title:'The Australian Animals Lesson',one:'One-of-a-kind friends Down Under.',
  seoTitle:'Australian Animals Lesson for Kids — Kangaroo, Koala, Wombat and Platypus, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 4–5: Australian animals — the hopping kangaroo with its pouch, the napping koala, the tunnelling wombat and the egg-laying platypus. Marsupials explained and a joey relay.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Australia&rsquo;s one-of-a-kind crew, one pouch at a time.',
  what:'Australia was cut off from the rest of the world for millions of years, so its animals grew up differently: kangaroos, koalas, wombats and the platypus exist wild nowhere else.',
  know:'Kangaroos hop in giant bounds and carry their joey in a pouch; koalas sleep up to twenty hours a day and eat only eucalyptus leaves; wombats dig tunnels and produce cube-shaped droppings; the platypus is a furry, duck-billed mammal that LAYS EGGS.',
  mean:'Islands make one-offs: with no competition from the rest of the world, Australia evolved its own rules — most of this card is a marsupial, and one is a living puzzle.',
  why:'The platypus alone is the best science story a child can meet: when scientists first saw a specimen, they checked it for stitches, sure it was a fake.',
  dev:'Map work (Australia, Down Under), classification (marsupial = pouch bearer), and the wonder of rule-breaking animals.',
  vocab:'Australia, marsupial, pouch, joey, kangaroo, koala, eucalyptus, wombat, burrow, platypus',
  teach:'1) The kangaroo: the hopper — big back legs, balance tail, and the mother&rsquo;s pouch carries the joey for months. Hopping is cheaper than running for long distances.\n2) The koala: the napper — up to twenty hours a day, because eucalyptus leaves are low-energy food and take all day to digest.\n3) The wombat: the digger — tunnels with multiple bedrooms, and a backwards-facing pouch so the dirt does not fill it while digging.\n4) The platypus: the rule-breaker — a mammal with a duck bill that lays eggs, hunts with its eyes shut (it senses electricity!) and has venomous ankle spurs. The scientists&rsquo; fake-check story is true.\n5) The map: find Australia — an island continent where the rules went their own way.',
  say:'"Australian animals are one-of-a-kind! The kangaroo hops with a joey in the pouch, the koala naps twenty hours a day, the wombat digs long tunnels, and the platypus — a mammal that LAYS EGGS! Australia plays by its own rules!"',
  notice:'On the card, the kangaroo balances on its big tail, the koala grips its branch, the wombat stands low and sturdy, and the platypus shows both the duck bill and the beaver-ish tail. Only one of the four is NOT a marsupial — find it.',
  ask:'"What is a marsupial?" (a mammal that carries its baby in a pouch) "Which animal on the card lays eggs?" (the platypus) "Why does the koala sleep so much?" (eucalyptus is low-energy food)',
  childSay:'Your child might hop everywhere with a toy in an apron-pouch, nap with koala pride ("twenty hours, that&rsquo;s my goal"), or fact-drop the cube-shaped wombat poop at dinner.',
  real:'Australia is a long way for most families — but every big zoo keeps kangaroos, and koala meet-and-greets run in Queensland. The platypus story retells beautifully: check for stitches.',
  play:'Joey relay: hop cushion-to-cushion with a stuffed animal tucked in an apron pouch — drop the joey, back one rock. Then the koala nap: twenty seconds of stillness on a "branch" (the arm of the sofa). Count it in eucalyptus leaves.',
  check:'Ask: "What is a baby kangaroo called?" (a joey — it rides in the pouch) "Which Australian animal lays eggs?" (the platypus) "What does the koala eat?" (eucalyptus leaves)'},
 'whales-and-dolphins':{
  title:'The Whales and Dolphins Lesson',one:'Ocean giants that breathe air.',
  seoTitle:'Whales and Dolphins Lesson for Kids — Whale, Dolphin, Manatee and Dugong, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 3+: whales and dolphins — the biggest animal alive, the whistling dolphin and the two gentle sea cows. Mammals not fish, the famous spout, and a whale-song telephone.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the ocean&rsquo;s air-breathing giants, one spout at a time.',
  what:'Whales and dolphins are mammals, not fish — they breathe air with lungs, feed their babies milk, and must come up for breath, which is why they blow the famous spout.',
  know:'The blue whale is the biggest animal that has ever lived; dolphins whistle name-like calls and sleep with half their brain awake; manatees graze seagrass like underwater cows; dugongs are the sailors&rsquo; "mermaids".',
  mean:'Mammal is the family word: hair (a little), milk, live babies, air to breathe — the ocean&rsquo;s giants are our relatives, not the fish&rsquo;s.',
  why:'The biggest animal ever on one card, plus the mermaid mix-up — this is the deck&rsquo;s easiest wow, and the ocean-conservation talk starts here.',
  dev:'Classification (mammal vs fish), size-scale wonder, and listening skills through whale-song play.',
  vocab:'whale, dolphin, spout, mammal, breath, manatee, dugong, seagrass, calf, song',
  teach:'1) The mammal rule: whales and dolphins are not fish — no gills, lungs instead; no eggs, live babies; and milk for the calf.\n2) The spout: the breath made visible — warm air blasts out the blowhole when they surface. Count the spouts on a boat trip.\n3) The whale: the blue whale&rsquo;s heart is the size of a small car, and its song travels across whole oceans.\n4) The dolphin: the whistler — each has a signature whistle, like a name. They call each other by it.\n5) The sea cows: the manatee and dugong graze seagrass meadows slowly all day; sailors who glimpsed them from ships wrote home about mermaids.',
  say:'"Whales and dolphins are mammals — like us! They breathe air and feed their babies milk. The whale&rsquo;s heart is as big as a car, the dolphin whistles its own name, and the sea cows munch seagrass all day. Up for air — WHOOSH, the spout!"',
  notice:'On the card, the whale arcs with its spout, the dolphin rides the wave, and the round-faced manatee and dugong drift below — the two sea cows side by side. Which one has the biggest mouth on the card?',
  ask:'"Is a whale a fish?" (no — a mammal, like us) "How does a dolphin have a name?" (a signature whistle) "Which two animals are the sea cows?" (the manatee and the dugong)',
  childSay:'Your child might answer every phone call with a whale song, sign birthday cards from "the dugong", or correct the mermaid record: "sailors saw SEA COWS, actually."',
  real:'Whale-watching boats run on the US, Canadian and UK coasts — the spout is the first sighting. Manatee parks in Florida let families watch sea cows float; dugongs live off Australia.',
  play:'Whale-song telephone: two paper cups joined by string — hum a long whale note, the listener answers with their own. Then dolphin jumps between two cushions, and a manatee float on the rug, slow as seagrass.',
  check:'Ask: "What comes out of the blowhole?" (air — the spout) "What do manatees eat?" (seagrass) "Are whales and dolphins fish?" (no — mammals)'},
 'dinosaurs':{
  title:'The Dinosaurs Lesson',one:'The giants of long, long ago.',
  seoTitle:'Dinosaurs Lesson for Kids — T-rex, Triceratops, Stegosaurus and Brachiosaurus, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 3+: dinosaurs — the T-rex&rsquo;s giant bite, the triceratops&rsquo; three horns, the stegosaurus&rsquo; plates and the tall brachiosaurus. How we know they lived, and birds as living dinosaurs.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the ancient giants and how we know about them, one fossil at a time.',
  what:'Dinosaurs were the giant reptiles that ruled the Earth for over 160 million years — long before people. We know them from fossils: bones turned to stone.',
  know:'The T-rex had teeth the size of bananas; the triceratops carried three horns and a bony frill; the stegosaurus wore back plates and had a brain about the size of a plum; the brachiosaurus stood as tall as a four-story building. And the biggest twist: birds ARE dinosaurs — living ones.',
  mean:'Deep time is the idea: 65 million years is too long to imagine, but a fossil you can touch makes it real — the past leaves receipts.',
  why:'Dinosaurs are the gateway drug to science: the child who learns "fossils tell the story" has the whole method in miniature.',
  dev:'Deep-time talk in kid units, evidence-based thinking (bones → scientists → story), and the bird connection that rewires everything.',
  vocab:'dinosaur, fossil, paleontologist, T-rex, triceratops, stegosaurus, brachiosaurus, extinct, bones, ancient',
  teach:'1) Start with the word: dinosaur means "terrible lizard" — but they were not lizards, and the T-rex was more like a giant bird cousin.\n2) The T-rex: the famous hunter — teeth like bananas, jaws like a truck. It lived about 66 million years ago.\n3) The triceratops: the three-horned plant-eater — its frill was a shield and its horns said "not today".\n4) The stegosaurus: the plated one — back plates for show and warmth, tail spikes for defense, and a plum-sized brain that managed fine.\n5) The brachiosaurus: the tall one — a four-story reach, front legs longer than back, eating treetops no one else could.\n6) The ending AND the twist: an asteroid ended the age 65 million years ago — but birds are living dinosaurs, so the dinosaurs never fully left.',
  say:'"Dinosaurs ruled the Earth long, long before people! The T-rex had banana-sized teeth, the triceratops had three horns, the stegosaurus wore plates, and the brachiosaurus was as tall as a house. And here is the secret: birds are living dinosaurs!"',
  notice:'On the card, the T-rex grins with its banana teeth, the triceratops shows all three horns, the stegosaurus carries its back plates, and the brachiosaurus reaches past the top of its square. Which one ate plants? Three of the four did!',
  ask:'"How do we know dinosaurs existed?" (fossils — bones turned to stone) "Which dinosaur was the tallest?" (the brachiosaurus) "Are there any dinosaurs alive today?" (yes — birds!)',
  childSay:'Your child might roar-banana at the dinner table, excavate a "fossil" (toy buried in rice) with a paintbrush like a real paleontologist, or refuse to eat chicken without acknowledging its family history.',
  real:'Natural history museums across the US, UK and Canada hang real skeletons — the brachiosaurus-in-the-lobby is a rite of passage. Fossil digs and dino-trail parks let children brush real stone.',
  play:'Dino stomp parade: giant slow steps around the room, one call per corner — ROAR (T-rex), snort (triceratops), clap-the-plates (stegosaurus), reach-the-ceiling (brachiosaurus). The stomp ends at the fossil bed (the couch).',
  check:'Ask: "What are dinosaur bones called when they turn to stone?" (fossils) "What happened to the dinosaurs?" (an asteroid ended their age) "Which animals today are dinosaurs?" (birds)'},
 'night-animals':{
  title:'The Night Animals Lesson',one:'The world after bedtime.',
  seoTitle:'Night Animals Lesson for Kids — Bat, Raccoon, Hedgehog and Firefly, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 4–5: night animals — the echo-seeing bat, the clever-handed raccoon, the rolling hedgehog and the blinking firefly. Echolocation explained and a flashlight firefly game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the night shift, one glow at a time.',
  what:'Night animals (nocturnal animals) sleep by day and wake at dark: the bat, the raccoon, the hedgehog and the firefly own the hours after bedtime.',
  know:'Bats are the only mammals that truly fly and they "see" with sound — echolocation; raccoons wash their food with incredibly sensitive hands; hedgehogs roll into a ball of a thousand spikes; fireflies blink in code to find each other.',
  mean:'Different clock, same needs: the night crew hunts, eats and raises babies exactly like the day crew — just in the dark, with night-ready tools.',
  why:'The night is the child&rsquo;s great mystery — the light goes off and the world disappears. This card says: it did not disappear; the other crew clocked in.',
  dev:'Sensory science (echolocation as sound-pictures), night-safety comfort (the dark has friendly workers), and gentle after-dark ethics.',
  vocab:'night, nocturnal, bat, echo, raccoon, hedgehog, firefly, blink, signal, dark',
  teach:'1) The word: nocturnal means "active at night" — the night shift works while we sleep.\n2) The bat: shouts a tiny shout and listens for the bounce — sound pictures map the dark better than eyes. It is the only mammal that truly flies.\n3) The raccoon: the hands — its paws are packed with touch sensors, and it dips food in water to "see" it with its fingers.\n4) The hedgehog: the rolling fortress — a thousand spikes, one muscle trick, and the ball beats every predator that can&rsquo;t hug.\n5) The firefly: the lantern — each blink is a message in light: "here I am, this way". Every species has its own blink code.',
  say:'"When we sleep, the night animals wake! The bat sees with sound — shout, listen, map! The raccoon feels with clever fingers, the hedgehog rolls into a spike-ball, and the firefly blinks hello in the dark. The night has its own crew!"',
  notice:'On the card, the bat spreads both wings, the raccoon shows its masked face and clever paws, the hedgehog bristles round, and the firefly glows above its own shadow. Which one would you hear first in the dark?',
  ask:'"How does a bat see in the dark?" (echoes — shout and listen for the bounce) "What does the hedgehog do when it is scared?" (rolls into a ball) "What is the firefly&rsquo;s blink for?" (a light message — here I am)',
  childSay:'Your child might shout-and-point games all week ("echolocation!"), roll gently into hedgehog balls on the rug, or request a firefly-blink goodnight code of their own.',
  real:'Summer nights in North America glow with real fireflies; UK gardens run hedgehog highways under fences; bat walks at nature centers listen to real echoes through bat detectors.',
  play:'Flashlight fireflies: dim the room, one torch per person, and blink messages across the dark — long-short-long means "come here" in firefly code. Then one hedgehog roll on the soft rug and a bat-round of blindfolded shouting-and-pointing.',
  check:'Ask: "What is it called when animals are active at night?" (nocturnal) "How does the bat find its way?" (echoes) "What does the raccoon use its hands for?" (feeling and washing food)'},
 'rainforest-animals':{
  title:'The Rainforest Animals Lesson',one:'Life at the treetop.',
  seoTitle:'Rainforest Animals Lesson for Kids — Parrot, Toucan, Sloth and Tree Frog, Step by Step (Animal Categories)',
  metaDescription:'A simple animal-categories lesson for ages 4–5: rainforest animals — the rainbow parrot, the banana-beaked toucan, the slow-motion sloth and the sticky-toed tree frog. Treetop life explained and a sloth speed race.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the rainforest&rsquo;s high-life crew, one branch at a time.',
  what:'Rainforests are the busiest forests on Earth, and most of their life happens high in the canopy: the parrot, the toucan, the sloth and the tree frog all live their whole lives in the treetops.',
  know:'Parrots crack the hardest nuts with hooked beaks and can live 50+ years; the toucan&rsquo;s beak is as long as its body but light as a sponge inside; sloths move so slowly that algae grows on their fur as camouflage; tree frogs climb with sticky toe-pads.',
  mean:'Layers are the idea: the rainforest is a tower of homes — floor, understory, canopy — and this card&rsquo;s crew owns the top floor.',
  why:'The rainforest holds more animal kinds than anywhere on Earth — and this card is the child&rsquo;s first visit to the canopy, where half of them live.',
  dev:'Layer thinking (a forest with floors), speed contrasts (sloth vs frog), and rainforest-conservation seeds: the trees ARE the homes.',
  vocab:'rainforest, canopy, treetop, parrot, toucan, sloth, tree frog, camouflage, sticky, layers',
  teach:'1) The tower: a rainforest has floors — dark floor, busy middle, sunny canopy. This crew lives at the top.\n2) The parrot: the nutcracker — a hooked beak cracks what no teeth could, and the colors shout "I live here" across the canopy.\n3) The toucan: the beak — banana-long but light as a sponge inside, it reaches fruit on twig-ends no other bird trusts.\n4) The sloth: the slow champion — moving slowly means nobody notices you; the green algae on its fur is the final camouflage. Slow is the superpower.\n5) The tree frog: the gripper — sticky toe-pads hold on through the rain, and some are no bigger than a thumb.',
  say:'"The rainforest is a tower of homes — and this crew lives at the top! The parrot cracks the hardest nuts, the toucan reaches fruit with its banana beak, the sloth moves in slow motion, and the tree frog clings with sticky toes!"',
  notice:'On the card, the parrot perches rainbow-bright, the toucan balances its huge beak, the sloth hangs relaxed, and the tree frog grips its leaf. The parrot is a repeat visitor — it also flew with the birds card!',
  ask:'"Where in the rainforest do these animals live?" (the treetops — the canopy) "Why does the sloth move so slowly?" (slow means unnoticed — camouflage) "What helps the tree frog climb?" (sticky toe-pads)',
  childSay:'Your child might hold a sloth-speed race and lose on purpose, banana-beak reach for the highest shelf snacks, or stick every climbable surface with toe-pad stickers.',
  real:'Rainforest rooms in every major zoo hold all four — the sloth encounter is the card&rsquo;s slow-motion highlight. Real rainforests: the Amazon in South America, and smaller ones across the world.',
  play:'Sloth speed race: chair to couch, as slowly as possible, no stopping — the LAST one home is the champion sloth. Then five frog-jumps for contrast, and a toucan reach: the highest fruit on the shelf, one arm.',
  check:'Ask: "What is the rainforest&rsquo;s top floor called?" (the canopy) "What grows on a sloth&rsquo;s fur?" (algae — green camouflage) "Which rainforest animal also lives on the birds card?" (the parrot)'}
};


